                { stageId: "med-s7f", topic: "Teach: SA Node", module: "Assessment", component: "FeynmanPrompt", skin: "Scientific", config: { data: { prompt: "Explain why the SA node is called the 'natural pacemaker' and what would happen if it failed.", sampleAnswer: "The SA node fires automatically at 60-100 bpm, setting the heart's rhythm. If the SA node fails, the AV node can take over as a backup pacemaker at a slower rate (40-60 bpm). If both fail, the ventricles can generate their own escape rhythm at 20-40 bpm. Each backup is slower — that's why artificial pacemakers are implanted when the natural conduction system fails.", fixedFeedback: "Superb explanation! You correctly described the hierarchy of pacemakers: SA node → AV node → ventricular escape rhythm, each progressively slower. This backup system is a brilliant evolutionary safety feature. Understanding this hierarchy is essential for clinical cardiology. Well done!" }, initialState: {} }, validation: { type: "exact", condition: {} }, feedback: { success: "Great teaching!", error: "The heart has backup pacemakers if the SA node fails.", hint: "The AV node and ventricles can generate their own rhythms." } }
