                { stageId: "med-s8f", topic: "Teach: ECG Reading", module: "Assessment", component: "FeynmanPrompt", skin: "Scientific", config: { data: { prompt: "Explain what the QRS complex represents and why it appears so much larger than the P wave on an ECG.", sampleAnswer: "The QRS complex represents ventricular depolarization — the electrical signal spreading through the ventricles causing them to contract. It's much larger than the P wave because the ventricles have much more muscle mass than the atria. More muscle generates a stronger electrical signal, producing a bigger waveform on the ECG. The P wave is small because the atria are thin-walled chambers with less tissue.", fixedFeedback: "Excellent explanation! You correctly connected muscle mass to wave amplitude. The ventricles are indeed much more muscular than the atria, generating a larger electrical signal. Also note that atrial repolarization is hidden within the QRS complex — it's too small to see separately. Great understanding of ECG principles!" }, initialState: {} }, validation: { type: "exact", condition: {} }, feedback: { success: "Great teaching!", error: "Think about the difference in muscle mass.", hint: "More muscle = bigger electrical signal." } }
